---

Scope of the Reference Implementation

This release includes:

- structural intelligence admissibility definitions
- deterministic five-state admissibility resolution
- conflict-safe abstention behavior
- deterministic admissibility demonstrations
- structural replay invariants
- reference traces and reproducibility demonstrations

SRI defines a deterministic structural model where intelligence admissibility emerges from structure:

resolve(structure) ->

- RESOLVED if structure is complete AND consistent
- INCOMPLETE if structure is incomplete
- ABSTAIN if admissibility conditions are unsatisfied
- CONFLICT if structure is contradictory
- FORBIDDEN if structure is explicitly blocked

---

What This Demonstrates

- deterministic intelligence admissibility
- admissibility before AI execution
- deterministic abstention
- conflict-safe intelligence gating
- forbidden-state structural blocking
- independence from inference as the source of admissibility
- identical structure produces identical admissibility states

---

Conformance

An implementation is considered compatible if it preserves:

- structure-based admissibility
- deterministic resolution
- deterministic abstention behavior
- replay-safe admissibility
- structure-driven intelligence visibility
- reproducibility of admissibility states under equivalent inputs
- explicit handling of incomplete, conflicting, abstained, and forbidden structure

Conformance is judged solely by observable behavior — not by internal implementation.

S1 = S2 -> State1 = State2

---

Conflict & Abstention Handling

In this reference implementation:

- CONFLICT is explicitly defined and demonstrated
- INCOMPLETE corresponds to structurally insufficient declarations
- ABSTAIN corresponds to unresolved admissibility conditions
- FORBIDDEN corresponds to explicit structural prohibition

Conformance requires:

- no forced intelligence under incomplete structure
- no coherent intelligence under conflicting structure
- no unsafe admissibility under forbidden structure
- no hallucinated admissibility under abstained structure

---

Deterministic Replay Guarantee

Admissibility remains invariant under replay:

same structure -> same admissibility state

This ensures:

replay != divergence

Admissibility depends only on structure — not inference variation.

---

Structural Admissibility Principle

SRI demonstrates:

structure -> admissibility -> intelligence visibility

without requiring training or probabilistic inference as the source of admissibility.

This enables:

- structure-first intelligence gating
- deterministic abstention
- replay-safe admissibility
- conflict-safe intelligence systems
- admissibility before AI execution

---

Relationship to Shunyaya Framework

SRI is part of the broader Shunyaya framework.

It builds on:

- SLANG -> correctness without execution
- ORL -> correctness without ordering
- STIME -> correctness without synchronized time
- STINT -> correctness without connectivity
- STILE -> correctness without communication
- SVARE -> correctness without computation
- STOCRS -> correctness without sequence or synchronization
- STOCRS-R -> reusable deterministic structural application evolution
- SRI -> intelligence admissibility before AI execution

Each removes a dependency.
Correctness or admissibility remains preserved by structure.

